.TH tlsget 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
tlsget \- fetch a page over TLS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tlsget
.I "address name path"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tlsget
opens a TLS connection to
.IR address ,
presents
.I name
for SNI and for checking the certificate, asks for
.IR path ,
and writes what comes back to standard output.
.PP
The address and the name are
.I separate arguments
on purpose.  Keeping the resolver out of it makes this a test of the
connection and of nothing else.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# tlsget 104.20.23.154 example.com /
.fi
.SH NOTES
Out of the box a TLS session here is encrypted but
.I not authenticated:
nobody in the middle can read it, but anybody in the middle can pretend
to be the far end.
.BR tlsca (1)
turns the checking on.
.PP
Set the clock first with
.BR ntpdate (1).
A machine that thinks it is 1970 rejects every certificate there is.
.PP
A failure is reported as a refused connection whatever went wrong \- a
bad certificate, an unreachable host and an unsupported cipher all look
the same from here.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tlsca (1),
.BR htget (1),
.BR ntpdate (1),
.BR dig (1)
